<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Confucianism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Confucian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Confucianism is a system of ethical, social and political thought based on the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, a Chinese philosopher of the fifth century BCE. It stresses hierarchy, filial piety, benevolent government and education. Adopted as state orthodoxy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> and revived as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, it underpinned the civil service of the Ming and Qing empires and influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confucianism.jpg" alt="Confucianism" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zhangzhugang · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiangyin_wenmiao_dachengdian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
